#Library
The best option is rdagger/micropython-ili9341 — it supports both the ILI9341 display and the XPT2046 touch controller.
Download these files and copy them to your Pico (via Thonny):

ili9341.py
xpt2046.py
xglcd_font.py (optional, for fonts)


#Wiring
| TJCTM24024-SPI Pin | Pico GPIO | Notes              |
|--------------------|-----------|--------------------|
| VCC                | 3V3       |                    |
| GND                | GND       |                    |
| CS                 | GP13      | TFT chip select    |
| RESET              | GP14      |                    |
| D/C                | GP15      |                    |
| SDI (MOSI)         | GP7       | SPI0 TX            |
| SCK                | GP6       | SPI0 SCK           |
| LED                | 3V3       | Backlight always on|
| SDO (MISO)         | —         | Not needed         |
| T_CLK              | GP10      | SPI1 SCK           |
| T_CS               | GP12      | Touch chip select  |
| T_DIN              | GP11      | SPI1 TX            |
| T_DO               | GP8       | SPI1 RX            |
| T_IRQ              | GP0       | Touch interrupt    |

Tips

Baudrate: 40MHz works well for the display; keep the touch SPI at 1MHz (XPT2046 is slow).
3.3V logic only — never connect signal lines to 5V or you'll damage the display.
Thonny is the easiest way to transfer .py files to the Pico — just save directly to the device.
If the screen stays white/blank, check your DC and CS pins first — those are the most common wiring mistakes.